WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.969
-Our next guest is a very
talented comedian and actor

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.004
you know from shows
such as "Detroiters,"

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.205
"Veep, and "Ted Lasso."

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:07.840
He stars in "The Afterparty."

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.743
New episodes are available
Fridays on Apple TV+.

00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:11.844
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:16.015
-I look at that picture,
and it hit me.

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:16.916
[ Thud ]
[ Grunts ]

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.318
[ Tires screech ]
-[Bleep]

00:00:18.318 --> 00:00:19.352
Holy [bleep]
Are you okay?

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:20.353
Don't be dead.
Don't be dead.

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.522
Don't be dead.
Hello! Aniq?

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.323
-Oh.
-Here we go. You okay?

00:00:24.323 --> 00:00:26.559
-I think I swallowed a molar,
but I'm fine.

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:28.561
I'm fine.
I got to get to that party.

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:30.329
-No, no, we got to get you
to a hospital, okay?

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:31.864
-No.

00:00:31.864 --> 00:00:33.032
Ooh.

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:36.069
Zoe and me, we had a moment.
We almost kissed.

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:37.503
-Oh, that's --
see, that's incr--

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:38.604
It almost makes you forget

00:00:38.604 --> 00:00:40.840
that you just got run over
by a car, right?

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.377
-Please welcome back to the show
Sam Richardson, everybody.

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.379
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:52.185 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:57.957 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.957 --> 00:00:59.825
How are you?
-I'm so good.

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:01.494
I'm very well.
-Welcome back to the show.

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:04.430
This is a fantastic premise
for a TV show.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.100
It is an afterparty
at a high school reunion,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.767
and someone's been murdered,

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:11.203
and each episode shows it
from a different perspective.

00:01:11.203 --> 00:01:12.805
It's got
a fantastic comedy cast.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:13.906
It must have been
so much fun to do.

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:15.274
-Oh, it's so much fun.

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:19.779
I mean, from top to bottom,
you know, Tiffany Haddish,

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:22.414
Ben Schwartz, Ilana Glazer,

00:01:22.414 --> 00:01:26.252
Jamie Demetriou,
just wall to wall.

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:30.189
Everybody in this thing
is the funniest, nicest,

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:32.959
best person to work with,

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:36.562
so it just kind of made it
just a joy and delight

00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:38.030
to go to work every day.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.298
-And especially to make --

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:41.834
anyone making comedy
during the pandemic,

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:43.937
which was a time
that was so lonely,

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:45.337
to be able to get together,

00:01:45.337 --> 00:01:47.340
obviously on a very controlled,
very safe set,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.941
to be with comedians again
must have been great.

00:01:48.941 --> 00:01:52.545
-Very safe.
We tested twice every day,

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:56.315
a rapid test and a PCR test,
and then we'd test on Sundays,

00:01:56.315 --> 00:01:57.984
so it was very safe.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:02:02.121
And then when we're on set,
it was so crazy

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:06.192
to, like, take your mask off
and then perform a scene.

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:08.995
You're like, "Oh, my God,
is this real life?"

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:10.930
But, like, in this real life,
somebody had been murdered,

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:14.701
So it was a little bit of --
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:17.370
You're like, "Would I trade
a murder to not wear a mask?"

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.274
-Hmm.
Am I the one who's dead?

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.909
-Hey, your co-star
Ike Barinholtz and our friend

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:27.546
was here on Monday,
and he left a signed headshot

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:29.882
he wanted me to give
David Letterman yesterday.

00:02:29.882 --> 00:02:31.784
I didn't.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:33.919
-But he wrote, "Dave,
thanks for all the laughs.

00:02:33.919 --> 00:02:35.822
Ike Barinholtz."
Would you like it?

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:38.825
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:40.660
-I mean, you don't have
anything else to do with this?

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.928
-Nothing.

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:43.696
-It's got plain paper
on the back, though, yeah?

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:44.864
-Oh, yeah,
I could use it for notes.

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:46.632
-Yeah, use that for notes.

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:47.734
-Thank you.
-Yeah, of course.

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:48.801
-You're really good at this.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:51.103
-I'm good at problem solving,
you know.

00:02:51.103 --> 00:02:53.139
-I mean, I was like, this is
just going to be garbage,

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:55.174
but now it's useful.

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:57.109
Have you -- You know,
the premise of this

00:02:57.109 --> 00:02:59.845
is people getting together,
old high school people.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:01.547
Have you gone to high school
reunions of your own?

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:03.215
-I haven't.

00:03:03.215 --> 00:03:06.185
This year would have been
my 20th reunion.

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.321
-That's a good one.
-Yeah.

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:10.456
I figured that would be
a really great one,

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:11.624
but I've always missed,

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.292
and this year I would have liked
to have gone,

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:15.561
because I think
I would want to go

00:03:15.561 --> 00:03:18.597
and just, like, be a full, like,
movie star.

00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:19.732
-Yeah, this is the year.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:23.002
-And just, like,
wear sunglasses throughout.

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:26.139
Back in Detroit, like,
"What's going on?"

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:28.407
-I can't imagine Detroit
would treat you very kindly

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:29.942
if you behaved that way.
-No, no.

00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:32.678
It's a real humbling place.
Like, "What are you doing?"

00:03:32.678 --> 00:03:35.882
Like, "Sorry.
I had cataracts."

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:39.452
-You also -- Is it true you did
Model U.N. in high school?

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:40.586
-I did.

00:03:40.586 --> 00:03:42.521
-So they would have been excited
to see you.

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:43.989
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah, yeah, alright.

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:44.957
-Yeah, a lot of U.N.-ers.

00:03:44.957 --> 00:03:46.225
-A lot of MUNers.
We call it MUN.

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:47.960
-Do you think they would be
disappointed to find out

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.995
you weren't in the actual U.N.?

00:03:49.995 --> 00:03:51.030
-Yeah, probably.
-Yeah.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.431
-I'd be like,
"I'm working on it."

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:54.467
But, you know,
I was in Model U.N.

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:55.501
I was in debate,

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.403
I was in a lot
of extracurriculars.

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.104
But I was, like, a bad student

00:03:59.104 --> 00:04:02.141
and, like, didn't really
work hard at stuff.

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:05.011
So, like, in Model U.N.,
I was always Ghana,

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.278
'cause my family's from Ghana,

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:07.746
and I would be like,
"Yeah, I'm Ghana.

00:04:07.746 --> 00:04:09.382
So what's the resolution?

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:12.618
Yeah, Ghana agrees.
Sure."

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:14.187
-So your heart wasn't in it.

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:16.288
-No. I just liked to hang out,
you know?

00:04:16.288 --> 00:04:18.023
-Well, that's nice.
That's a nice group of people.

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:19.992
The Model U.N.-ers are
a nice group of people
to hang out with.

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:22.261
But you did have politics
in your family.

00:04:22.261 --> 00:04:25.631
Your aunt was a member
of Congress, elected in 1990.

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:27.099
-Yes, the first black woman

00:04:27.099 --> 00:04:28.701
to be elected to Congress
in Michigan.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:30.269
-That's amazing.
-Yeah.

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:33.038
-And your dad
worked on her campaigns?

00:04:33.038 --> 00:04:35.308
-Yes, my dad
was her campaign manager,

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:37.176
and she just passed in November,

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:39.478
so rest in peace,
Auntie Barbara Rose.

00:04:39.478 --> 00:04:45.551
But my dad was her campaign
manager, and I was young,

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:48.287
so I would just be around
the meetings in the office,

00:04:48.287 --> 00:04:49.554
but, like, I wasn't
paying attention.

00:04:49.554 --> 00:04:51.090
I was like about 7, 8.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.059
You know, so I'd be --
[ Laughs ]

00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:55.628
I was just, like,
a real distraction.

00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:57.363
Like, one time I had --

00:04:57.363 --> 00:04:58.564
I would just, like,
listen to music.

00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:02.502
I had this boom box
and I'd play this tape,

00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:08.607
and it was the Chipmunks
singing "Bette Davis Eyes."

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:10.643
-Oh, yeah.
-And I'm just, like, there --

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.845
"Ooh, she'll take you
on the floor."

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:15.048
My dad was like, "What is that?"

00:05:15.048 --> 00:05:16.515
He comes in like,
"What is this thing?"

00:05:16.515 --> 00:05:17.716
It was too late.
It was already in my head.

00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:20.920
-Yeah, but those were the seeds
of politics

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.221
that brought you
to the Model U.N.

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:23.723
-Exactly.
-Yeah.

00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:26.892
Did Auntie Barbara Rose
watch your work?

00:05:26.892 --> 00:05:28.227
Did she watch "Veep,"
for example,

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:29.828
which was, you know,
a show that everybody says

00:05:29.828 --> 00:05:32.365
is the most like politics?

00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:35.868
-You know, if she did,
she didn't talk about it a lot.

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:37.169
-Uh-huh.

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:39.972
-One of my first
television gigs was,

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:42.408
I had a recurring role
on "The Office,"

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:46.045
and my family had,
like, a viewing party.

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:47.713
-As they should.
That's very sweet, supportive.

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:50.316
-Very sweet. At my
Auntie Barbara Rose's house.

00:05:50.316 --> 00:05:52.551
And so everybody went.
They watched the episode.

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:54.720
And I saw her after, and she was
like, "I saw your episode.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:55.821
You were hardly in it."

00:05:55.821 --> 00:06:00.459
I was like, "Well, yeah,
it's my first one.

00:06:00.459 --> 00:06:03.829
I don't just start at the top,
you know?"

00:06:03.829 --> 00:06:06.032
-Now, the first time I saw you,

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:08.968
you were on main stage
at Second City in Chicago,

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:10.469
but you had done
Second City Detroit.

00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:13.739
You had also done
Second City touring.

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:16.108
For people who don't know,
this is sort of a touring troupe

00:06:16.108 --> 00:06:19.912
of improvisers that play venues
of all sizes, of all types.

00:06:19.912 --> 00:06:22.315
What were some
of your weirder gigs?

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:24.350
-There was some really fun gigs.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:25.852
You know, you're playing
in opera houses.

00:06:25.852 --> 00:06:27.252
It's like,
oh, what a great audience.

00:06:27.252 --> 00:06:29.589
Then there were
some really trash ones,

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:34.459
like this one,
we were performing improv

00:06:34.459 --> 00:06:36.863
for a marathon.

00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:38.364
So...

00:06:38.364 --> 00:06:40.799
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.799 --> 00:06:42.034
[ Laughs ]

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:45.637
As confused as you are,
so were we, you know.

00:06:45.637 --> 00:06:47.607
-But wait.

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:49.842
Would you have to run alongside?

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:52.278
-That would make more sense.
-Okay.

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:54.846
-But we're just on this stage,
and the people are running past,

00:06:54.846 --> 00:06:57.416
and we're performing, like,
improv games and stuff.

00:06:57.416 --> 00:06:58.751
And, like, there's no way

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:01.120
you're going to get
the full thing of that.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.955
Like, you're either
at the beginning

00:07:02.955 --> 00:07:06.024
or the end of whatever joke
or bit you're doing.

00:07:06.024 --> 00:07:08.294
So it's like, "And that's how
they called them Slappy."

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:11.096
And they're like,
"What does that mean?"

00:07:11.096 --> 00:07:14.166
"Welcome to my office. I'm --"
And then that's it, you know?

00:07:14.166 --> 00:07:17.002
-So it was really only good
for very slow runners.

00:07:17.002 --> 00:07:19.037
-Exactly, the ones who are like,
"I think I'm done.

00:07:19.037 --> 00:07:20.873
Oh, comedy.
Very good."

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:24.143
-Now, did you have
a Disney World gig?

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:26.345
Or did you just aspire
to have one?

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:30.215
-So I aspired to have,
but then I went to --

00:07:30.215 --> 00:07:34.153
I worked in Disney World
in the college program.

00:07:34.153 --> 00:07:36.388
I went and did the college
program in Walt Disney World,

00:07:36.388 --> 00:07:41.093
and I ended up working
in Animal Kingdom.

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:42.494
-Uh-huh.

00:07:42.494 --> 00:07:43.462
[ Applause ]
-Yeah!

00:07:43.462 --> 00:07:46.098
Animal Kingdom, stand up!

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:49.301
Whoo!
My animalites are here.

00:07:49.301 --> 00:07:51.303
I worked on Dinosaur the ride.

00:07:51.303 --> 00:07:52.237
-Alright.
[ Applause ]

00:07:52.237 --> 00:07:55.441
-Whoo!
Dinosaur-heads, stand up.

00:07:55.441 --> 00:07:57.710
The dinosaur people are here.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:01.681
But it was a wild time,
like, altogether,

00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:03.482
because it was a college
program, so all I had to do

00:08:03.482 --> 00:08:07.286
was just, like, live in these
apartments that they had for us,

00:08:07.286 --> 00:08:09.922
and then all my time was just
spent going to Disney World

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:11.924
for, like, everything.
-Yeah.

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:13.358
-Like, 'cause what
other activities?

00:08:13.358 --> 00:08:17.029
Go to the grocery store?
No, I'm going up to Epcot.

00:08:17.029 --> 00:08:18.463
-Now, did you ultimately --

00:08:18.463 --> 00:08:21.267
have you exhausted your joy
of Disney World,

00:08:21.267 --> 00:08:22.501
having spent so much time there?

00:08:22.501 --> 00:08:27.105
-No, no.
I love that place.

00:08:27.105 --> 00:08:28.974
I went to Disney World --

00:08:28.974 --> 00:08:30.710
Before that,
I had been one time.

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:33.112
I went with my dad.
He took me to Disney World.

00:08:33.112 --> 00:08:34.980
It was just me and him,
and my dad was a little older,

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:39.818
so he was kind of tired,
so we'd go on the rides.

00:08:39.818 --> 00:08:41.253
But he'd be like, "I'm going to
sit down on this bench.

00:08:41.253 --> 00:08:42.355
You enjoy."

00:08:42.355 --> 00:08:44.189
And there were so many times

00:08:44.189 --> 00:08:46.859
where he was sticking to me,
like, "Don't be late.

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:49.295
Come back at this time.
Otherwise, trouble."

00:08:49.295 --> 00:08:51.230
And I would be late so often.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:53.199
But then on the way back,
I think I learned my lesson.

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:55.601
On the flight back,
we were at the airport

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:57.135
getting ready to fly back,

00:08:57.135 --> 00:08:59.271
and I was like,
"I'm gonna go get some water.

00:08:59.271 --> 00:09:00.706
I'll be right back."
"Don't be late."

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:02.474
And I was like, "I won't be.
I promise."

00:09:02.474 --> 00:09:04.042
Went and I was
reading a magazine.

00:09:04.042 --> 00:09:06.112
Uh-oh!
Came back, we missed the flight.

00:09:06.112 --> 00:09:07.413
[ Laughter ]

00:09:07.413 --> 00:09:10.349
So all the lessons of the week
were for nothing.

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:12.118
-I just think you wanted
to stay at Disney World.

00:09:12.118 --> 00:09:14.119
-Yeah, I was like,
"Let's get an apartment here."

00:09:14.119 --> 00:09:15.287
-Thanks so much for being here.

00:09:15.287 --> 00:09:16.355
It's always a pleasure
to see you.

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:17.790
You guys, that's Sam Richardson.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:19.258
New episodes of "The Afterparty"

00:09:19.258 --> 00:09:21.160
are available Fridays
on Apple TV+.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:22.528
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

